B\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mi\" transform=\"translate(888,0)\"><use data-c=\"1D435\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D435\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\" transform=\"translate(1647,0)\"><g data-mml-node=\"msub\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D439\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-I-1D439\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"mn\" transform=\"translate(676,-150) scale(0.707)\"><use data-c=\"34\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-34\"></use></g></g></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3115.6,0)\"><use data-c=\"29\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-29\"></use></g></g><g data-mml-node=\"mo\" transform=\"translate(3671.2,0)\"><use data-c=\"2E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-TEX-N-2E\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container> The precipitated diazonium fluoroborate is isolated, dried and heated untill decomposition occurs to yield the aryl fluoride. This reaction is known as <b>Balz-Schiemann reaction</b>. \n<br><br><img class=\"question-image\" src=\"https://imagex.cdn.examgoal.net/bGild1tiBnZylry8S/ZNv1wTdN37nmbN0xOq3FCGHMaio7U/I3M1mIAdsochm6AaIb3UFx/image.png\" loading=\"lazy\" alt=\"AIEEE 2010 Chemistry - Compounds Containing Nitrogen Question 238 English Explanation 2\"> "}]}